####################################################################
# clinicalEvents.ttl — HAND-AUTHORED. NOT GENERATED.
#
# Curated axioms deriving mda:ClinicalEvent knowledge (see ontology.ttl's
# "Clinical Event" section) — kept in its own file, separate from
# inference.ttl, because it is a genuinely different kind of clinical
# knowledge: inference.ttl's axioms establish an alarm's OWN technical/
# clinical interpretation (metric -> property -> process -> organ); this
# file's axioms interpret that already-derived state further, into a
# discrete acute event (e.g. an absent cardiac contraction rate -> cardiac
# arrest). Loaded everywhere inference.ttl is (see each consumer's own
# INFERENCE_PATH/CLINICAL_EVENTS_PATH pairing) so it participates in the
# same reasoning pass, not a parallel one.
#
# WHAT WORKS DECLARATIVELY HERE, AND WHY — READ BEFORE ADDING A RULE
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# The single-alarm axioms below (Cardiac arrest, Respiratory arrest, Reduced
# pulmonary function) are anchored on mda:PhysiologicalProcess (impliesClinicalEvent's domain), reached
# by walking BACKWARD through the already-existing Metric->approximates->
# Property->isPropertyOf->Process chain via inverse-property restrictions.
# mda:PhysiologicalProcess is Referential — one shared IRI per concept
# (e.g. physiologicalProcess:CardiacContraction), reused by every alarm,
# device, and patient that ever approximates it — which would be a real
# problem for a STATIC assertion reasoned once over a shared, accumulating
# graph (see that class's own section above). It is NOT a problem here,
# because 4_OPERATIONAL/op_knowledge.py's Timeline.situation_at/
# background_at reason over a FRESH graph per (patient, instant),
# containing only that patient's currently-relevant facts, then discard
# it — there is no shared, accumulating graph for a tag to leak across.
# See ontology.ttl's "RECONSIDERED AGAIN" note on mda:impliesClinicalEvent for
# the full reasoning (this property's domain used to be mda:Metric,
# specifically to avoid this generalisation, before that operational
# scoping argument was worked through).
#
# The concluding step still cannot mint a new, separately-identified
# individual — that limitation is about what OWL-RL can conditionally
# construct (rdf:type membership, or property edges composed from already-
# asserted links via propertyChainAxiom; never a conditional edge to a
# fixed, named individual), and is unaffected by which node impliesClinicalEvent
# lives on. It concludes a property VALUE (mda:impliesClinicalEvent) on the
# same Process individual the restriction is written over.
#
# mda:impliesClinicalEvent is tagged mda:situational in ontology.ttl, the same
# interpretive, alarm-duration-ONLY category mda:approximates/
# mda:isPropertyOf already use (and which already reaches Process in the
# existing extraction walk, so this rides along for free) — deliberately
# NOT mda:persistsPostAlarm, which is for facts with a physical reason to
# outlive their alarm (device connectivity, signal quality). A clinical
# event has no such reason: it is only true while the condition that
# asserted it (hasRate Absent) is still being reported. Verified directly:
# the entailed impliesClinicalEvent triple is present in Timeline.situation_at()
# while its triggering alarm is active, and gone within 1 second of that
# alarm ending — no grace period, confirmed empirically before trusting
# this, both under the original Metric anchoring and re-confirmed after
# the move to Process.
#
# A genuinely cross-alarm condition (cardiorespiratory arrest: Cardiac
# arrest and Respiratory arrest tagged on two DIFFERENT Process concepts)
# still does not fit the single-node pattern above — OWL-RL cannot combine
# facts from two otherwise-unconnected individuals, nor conditionally mint
# a third, and moving to Process-anchoring does not change that: the two
# tags sit on two different, unconnected Process concepts regardless. That
# coincidence is detected procedurally, by a plain SPARQL SELECT under
# CODE/evaluation_poc/reasoner/rules/ (cat3a_cardiorespiratory_arrest.rq)
# — no per-alarm anchor, just "are both tags present in the merged graph
# right now" — and mda_poc_assessment.py mints the actual mda:ClinicalEvent
# individual on a genuinely new coincidence (deduplicated so a coincidence
# that stays true across several alarm arrivals mints exactly one node,
# not one per check).
#
# Ventilation failure (clinicalEvent:VentilationFailure) is cross-alarm for
# a DIFFERENT reason: its two conditions are not just on different
# individuals, they are on different KINDS of node entirely. Reduced
# pulmonary function is a Process-anchored tag, exactly like Cardiac/
# Respiratory arrest above. The other half — "a mechanical ventilation
# alarm" — is a raw Device-level operation-state fact (mda:
# hasDeviceOperationState != opstate:Enabled on a device:MechanicalVentilator
# individual), not a clinical interpretation at all: it is deliberately NOT
# given its own impliesClinicalEvent-producing condition class here, the same
# way CAT1a/CAT2a match hasQualityState/hasCategory directly rather than
# routing a raw technical fact through this vocabulary — impliesClinicalEvent's
# domain is mda:PhysiologicalProcess specifically because a clinical event is
# an interpretation OF a physiological process's state, and a device fault
# has no such state to interpret. cat3b_ventilation_failure.rq therefore
# matches the device fault as a plain triple pattern and the physiological
# side via the ReducedPulmonaryFunction tag in the same SELECT, with minting
# handled the same way as cat3a's (mda_poc_assessment.py's
# mint_ventilation_failure(), same rising-edge dedup shape).
#
# NAMING DEVIATION FROM inference.ttl
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# inference.ttl keeps every restriction class anonymous (blank nodes) —
# deliberately here, the two condition classes below are NAMED
# (mda:CardiacArrestCondition/mda:RespiratoryArrestCondition) purely for
# traceability: this file's own axioms are few enough, and specific enough
# to one clinical concept each, that a name pays for itself in
# debuggability (a reasoner trace or a query plan reads "..a
# mda:CardiacArrestCondition.." instead of an anonymous node id) without
# the collision/reuse risk a shared, general-purpose restriction class
# would carry across inference.ttl's much larger axiom set. The concluded
# VALUE (clinicalEvent:CardiacArrest, a skos:Concept) is unchanged — only
# the triggering condition class gained a name.
####################################################################

@prefix mda:          <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#> .
@prefix owl:          <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:         <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix metric:       <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/metric/> .
@prefix metricRate:   <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/metric-rate/> .
@prefix clinicalEvent: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/clinical-event/> .


####################################################################
# Cardiac arrest
#
# Anchored on mda:PhysiologicalProcess (impliesClinicalEvent's domain — see
# ontology.ttl's "RECONSIDERED AGAIN" note for why this is safe despite
# PhysiologicalProcess being Referential), reached by walking BACKWARD
# through the already-existing chain via inverse-property restrictions:
# any process that is isPropertyOf-reached from a property that is
# approximates-reached from a Metric which is BOTH metric:HeartRate AND
# currently reports hasRate metricRate:Absent gets tagged impliesClinicalEvent
# CardiacArrest. Verified with an isolated reasoner test before writing
# this (positive: HeartRate+Absent -> tagged; negative: HeartRate+Normal
# -> untagged) — the inverse-restriction construct is heavier than
# anything else in this file (everything else here and in inference.ttl
# walks forward along asserted properties only), so it earned its own
# check rather than trusting the syntax to behave as expected.
#
# Scoping: this fires per PROCESS, not per metric or per alarm — deliberate
# now, not a residual risk. mda:PhysiologicalProcess is Referential (one
# shared IRI per concept, e.g. physiologicalProcess:CardiacContraction),
# so this reads as "this patient's current cardiac-contraction state
# includes an absent rate," regardless of which device or sensing pathway
# produced it — see ontology.ttl's note on why that generalisation is safe
# here specifically (per-tick, per-patient operational reasoning, never a
# static shared graph).
####################################################################

mda:CardiacArrestCondition a owl:Class ;
    owl:intersectionOf ( mda:PhysiologicalProcess
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty [ owl:inverseOf mda:isPropertyOf ] ;
          owl:someValuesFrom
            [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty [ owl:inverseOf mda:approximates ] ;
              owl:someValuesFrom
                [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( metric:HeartRate
                    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasRate ; owl:hasValue metricRate:Absent ] ) ] ] ] ) ;
    rdfs:subClassOf
      [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:impliesClinicalEvent ; owl:hasValue clinicalEvent:CardiacArrest ] .


####################################################################
# Respiratory arrest
#
# Same shape as Cardiac arrest above, substituting the respiratory
# pathway. Real corpus archetype: "PHILIPSMONITOR - Apneu" (see DATA/
# ANNOTATION/consensus/p75_annotated.csv) — RespirationRate, Absent.
####################################################################

mda:RespiratoryArrestCondition a owl:Class ;
    owl:intersectionOf ( mda:PhysiologicalProcess
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty [ owl:inverseOf mda:isPropertyOf ] ;
          owl:someValuesFrom
            [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty [ owl:inverseOf mda:approximates ] ;
              owl:someValuesFrom
                [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( metric:RespirationRate
                    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasRate ; owl:hasValue metricRate:Absent ] ) ] ] ] ) ;
    rdfs:subClassOf
      [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:impliesClinicalEvent ; owl:hasValue clinicalEvent:RespiratoryArrest ] .


####################################################################
# Reduced pulmonary function
#
# Same shape again, substituting a DECREASED reading for an ABSENT one, and
# metric:RespirationVolume_Minute (minute ventilation) for metric:
# RespirationRate — deliberately the phase-specific subclass, not the
# broader metric:RespirationVolume, which would also catch metric:
# RespirationVolume_Tidal + Decreased ("DATEXOHMEDACOMVENTILATOR - VTexp
# Low"). A low tidal volume alone can be rate-compensated and does not by
# itself carry the same clinical weight; minute ventilation — rate x tidal
# volume — is the textbook marker of inadequate ventilation, and is what
# the real archetype below actually reports. Same precedent CAT1b's own
# report already established for narrowing metric-match scope to the exact
# archetype signature rather than the broader process-level match (see
# cat1b_asystole_ibp.rq's header — that narrowing closed a real over-match,
# not a cosmetic one).
#
# This is a building-block condition, not raised as a standalone finding —
# see clinicalEvent:ReducedPulmonaryFunction's own definition — feeding
# clinicalEvent:VentilationFailure the same way Cardiac/Respiratory arrest
# feed CardioRespiratoryArrest.
#
# Real corpus archetype: "MEDIBUS - MV ondergrens" (see DATA/ANNOTATION/
# consensus/p75_annotated.csv) — RespirationVolume, Minute, Decreased.
####################################################################

mda:ReducedPulmonaryFunctionCondition a owl:Class ;
    owl:intersectionOf ( mda:PhysiologicalProcess
        [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty [ owl:inverseOf mda:isPropertyOf ] ;
          owl:someValuesFrom
            [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty [ owl:inverseOf mda:approximates ] ;
              owl:someValuesFrom
                [ a owl:Class ; owl:intersectionOf ( metric:RespirationVolume_Minute
                    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:hasRate ; owl:hasValue metricRate:Decreased ] ) ] ] ] ) ;
    rdfs:subClassOf
      [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty mda:impliesClinicalEvent ; owl:hasValue clinicalEvent:ReducedPulmonaryFunction ] .
